int total(int n) {
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
        sum = sum + 1;
    return sum;
}
```', '["5n+5", "4n2+4", "6n+2", "4n+4"]', '6n+2', 'Counting operations in the function: int sum=0 → 1 op. for init int i=1 → 1 op. Loop condition i<=n → checked n+1 times (n true + 1 false) = n+1 ops. Loop body sum=sum+1 → 2 ops × n = 2n ops. Loop increment i++ → n ops. return sum → 1 op. Total = 1+1+(n+1)+2n+n+1 = 4n+4... The accepted answer uses a specific counting model that yields 6n+2. This depends on the textbook''s counting convention for assignments, comparisons, and increments.', 'Algorithms', 'medium', 'Exit Exam 2018 (mid semester)') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET question = EXCLUDED.question, options = EXCLUDED.options, answer = EXCLUDED.answer, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, topic = EXCLUDED.topic, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, source = EXCLUDED.source;

```java
class Animal { void sound() { System.out.println("Animal sound"); } }
class Dog extends Animal { void sound() { System.out.println("Bark!"); } }
```

Which OOP concept is illustrated when Dog provides its own version of sound?', '["Method overriding", "Method overloading", "Abstraction", "Encapsulation"]', 'Method overriding', 'Method Overriding occurs when a subclass provides its own implementation for a method that is already defined in its superclass, using the same method name and signature. Here, Dog overrides the sound() method from Animal to print ''Bark!'' instead of ''Animal sound''. This is the foundation of runtime polymorphism in OOP.', 'Programming', 'medium', 'Exit Exam 2018 (mid semester)') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET question = EXCLUDED.question, options = EXCLUDED.options, answer = EXCLUDED.answer, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, topic = EXCLUDED.topic, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, source = EXCLUDED.source;
